"Radiobiology: Cell Survival and Fractionation: teaching notes",
"Radiobiology: Cell Survival and Fractionation",
"Junior and senior medical physics track (calculus); beside the radiation physics class, radiobiology unit",
"In the linear quadratic model the surviving fraction after a dose $$D$$ given at once is $$S = e^{-\\alpha D - \\beta D^2}$$. The initial slope of ln $$S$$ is $$-\\alpha$$, and $$\\alpha/\\beta$$ is the dose at which single track and two track killing are equal: {#} Gy for V79 cells in air.",
"Oxygen fixes radiation damage. Cells without oxygen need {#} to {#} times the dose (V79) for the same survival, and the oxygen enhancement ratio can rise or fall with dose depending on the cell line (T1: {#} at low dose falling to {#}). Half the effect is reached at a few mmHg, so it is tumor hypoxia, not the difference between venous and arterial blood, that matters.",
"A clonogenic assay measures survival as colonies per seeded cell, relative to the plating efficiency of unirradiated controls; counting errors are about $$\\sqrt{c}$$, so the seeding rises with dose to keep every flask countable.",
"Splitting a dose into $$n$$ fractions of $$d$$ gives $$\\mathrm{BED} = nd(1 + d/(\\alpha/\\beta))$$. Tissues with a small α/β (late responding normal tissue, about {#} Gy) are spared most by small fractions; a tumor with a large α/β (about {#} Gy) loses effect when the same late effect is given in fewer fractions, a prostate cancer with α/β {#} Gy gains.",
"The proton RBE is not a constant {#}: it rises with LET toward the end of the range, as the dose per fraction falls, and as α/β falls. At {#} keV/µm and {#} Gy per fraction on tissue with α/β {#} Gy the model gives {#}.",
"The strip across the top switches between four views. <strong>Survival and oxygen</strong> draws survival against dose for V79 hamster lung cells, T1 human kidney cells (mean literature α and β in air and in hypoxia, Wenzl and Wilkens {#}) or an α and β of your own, with the α and β parts, an oxygen pressure slider from {#} to {#} mmHg, the effect of oxygen against its pressure, and the OER against dose with its low and high dose limits. <strong>Clonogenic assay</strong> seeds three flasks, irradiates them and draws the colonies; the counts are simulated Poisson counts, and a mystery line hides its α, β and plating efficiency behind a code. <strong>Fractionation</strong> draws the treatment calendar, a BED staircase for α/β {#}, {#} and {#} Gy and one of your choosing, and the EQD2 against the number of fractions; Keep the late effect fixed makes the total dose follow $$n$$. <strong>Proton RBE</strong> draws the McNamara {#} RBE against LET_d with its RBE_max and RBE_min limits and Paganetti's {#} averages, the RBE against dose, and the photon dose for the same effect. Fourteen setups.",
"<strong>V79 in air and without oxygen</strong>: drag the dose and watch the OER bracket. Then <strong>OER at a high dose</strong> and <strong>Human kidney cells</strong>. Ask Q1.",
"<strong>A little oxygen goes a long way</strong>: move the pO₂ slider from {#} to {#} mmHg and then on to {#}. Ask Q2.",
